#: ../../guide/router.rst:3
msgid "Router"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:5
msgid "A :class:`~aiorubi.dispatcher.router.Router` groups handlers so a bot can be split into reusable modules — an admin module, a games module, a payments module — each mounted into the main dispatcher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:10
msgid "Creating and registering handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:27
msgid "Observers are the same as on the dispatcher: ``new_message``, ``updated_message``, ``removed_message``, ``inline_message``, ``started_bot``, ``stopped_bot`` and ``error`` / ``errors``, plus the lifecycle observers ``startup`` / ``shutdown``. All of them support the decorator and ``register`` forms and accept any :doc:`filters <filter>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:35
msgid "Including routers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:37
msgid "Routers form a tree. The dispatcher is always the root; child routers are visited after the parent's own handlers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:55
msgid "``include_router`` attaches the child (setting its read-only ``parent_router``), and nesting works to any depth:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:62
msgid "Both methods return the router, so chaining is possible. A :class:`RuntimeError` is raised when you try to include the dispatcher itself into anything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:67
msgid "Propagation order"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:69
msgid "When an update arrives it flows depth-first through the tree in registration order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:72
msgid "outer middlewares of the matching observer, from root to leaf;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:73
msgid "the root router's handlers (top to bottom);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:74
msgid "each included router's handlers in inclusion order, recursing into their sub-routers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:77
msgid "The **first handler whose filters all pass** wins; the remaining routers never see the update unless the handler raises :class:`~aiorubi.dispatcher.event.bases.SkipHandler`. Register the most specific routers first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:83
msgid "Router-level filters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:85
msgid "``observer.filter(...)`` attaches filters to *every* handler of that observer, checked before the handler's own filters — ideal for guards:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:95
msgid "Router-level filters apply to all handlers registered on that observer of that router *and are not inherited* by included sub-routers (their observers are separate objects)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:100
msgid "Observer name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:102
msgid "Every observer has an ``event_name`` attribute; the ``resolve_used_update_types()`` method walks the whole tree and returns the event names that actually have handlers — polling and webhook helpers can use it to fetch only relevant update types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:108
msgid "Lifecycle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:118
msgid "Events emitted on the dispatcher (``dp.emit_startup()`` / ``dp.emit_shutdown()``) propagate through the whole tree, so each module manages its own resources."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:123
msgid "Chain helpers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:125
msgid "``router.chain_head`` — iterate from the root down to *this* router."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:126
msgid "``router.chain_tail`` — iterate from *this* router down to the last sub-router."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:129
msgid "These generators power the middleware resolution and the propagation walk; you rarely need them directly, but they are handy for debugging the router tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:134
msgid "Typical project layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/router.rst:161
msgid "Each module owns its filters, middlewares and lifecycle hooks — the entry point only wires them together."
msgstr ""

